WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest is
a five-time Emmy-nominated

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:04.537
and Golden Globe
Award-winning actress

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.205
you know for her portrayal
of Samantha Jones

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:08.341
in "Sex and the City."
She produces and stars

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.810
in the new show "Filthy Rich,"
which airs Mondays on Fox.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:11.978
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:14.280
-You will give up
this MMMA thing.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.649
-MMA?
-You are Jesús's only parent.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:19.185
And if you get hurt,
who will he have?

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:21.120
Besides, I would
very much like for you

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.822
to join my security team.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.623
-She just put you on staff, bro.

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:27.760
-It is an entry-level position
that you can build on.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.095
-Jason, wherever he is,

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:31.297
he will close
that ganja factory.

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.499
And...Ginger.
-Here it comes.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:36.869
-You and your mother,
you will leave New Orleans.

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:40.272
You will close that porn site
of yours, and you will never,

00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:43.810
ever speak to any member
of the press ever again.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.678
-Please welcome to the show
Kim Cattrall!

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.480
Thanks for being here, Kim.
How are you?

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.516
-I'm great. Nice to see you.
-Nice to see you, as well.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.485
And congratulations
are in order.

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:57.123
You are a new --
a brand-new U.S. citizen.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:01:02.662
-I am, proudly.
August 17th, I became a citizen.

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:05.899
I took the test and passed.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.600
There were six --
There were ten questions,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:08.968
and I got the first six right,

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:12.438
so they said, "Okay,
you can become a citizen."

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.840
-You really -- You joined us
at a great time, Kim.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.777
You really got in
while the gettin's good.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:20.980
-You need my vote.
-That's true.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.217
Welcome aboard, and we're
very happy to have you.

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:25.484
Before we get to your show,

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:27.853
which I am looking forward
to talking about,

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.521
we had a mutual friend --

00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:31.424
We've talked on this show

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.493
about his sad passing
due to COVID --

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:36.762
Hal Willner, who was
the music supervisor at "SNL."

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:39.899
But I did not realize you
were close with Hal, as well.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.736
-Yeah.
I met Hal through Lou Reed.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.672
They were doing the radio show
together, and we became,

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:50.442
you know, really close
within that circle of friends.

00:01:50.442 --> 00:01:53.345
He was the first person
that we lost to COVID,

00:01:53.345 --> 00:01:55.982
so it was really sad.

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.184
My partner and I, Russ,
we were in New York

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.053
till about end of April.

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:03.789
And then we got on a plane
and went up to Vancouver Island

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:06.659
where my mom is
to make sure she'd be okay.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.229
And I brought
this little mini pod

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.232
that, Hal,
a few birthdays before,

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:17.203
had put over 2,000 songs
of his music on for me.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:20.672
So I felt -- You know,
we were all grieving Hal,

00:02:20.672 --> 00:02:23.443
but every morning,
every afternoon,

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:26.646
I just had his music
in the room in the house,

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:28.414
so that was comforting.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.783
-He was a man of so many skills,

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.852
but that ability
to curate a musical list

00:02:33.852 --> 00:02:37.190
based on the taste
of his friends was really --

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.125
it was -- it was something else.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:41.127
He will definitely be missed.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.996
You -- You have
a wonderful, new show.

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:47.132
You play -- This is about
a very rich family in the South

00:02:47.132 --> 00:02:49.168
who has a Christian TV network.

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:52.071
And we learn pretty early on --
A lot happens in the pilot.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.206
That the patriarch
of this family, your husband,

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:55.774
is maybe not living
to the values

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:58.210
that he espouses on television.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.378
-That's right.

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:01.681
-Are you enjoying the show
so far?

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:05.017
-Oh, my God, yes.
It's great to play a woman,

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:09.289
you know, who has that
most feminine of qualities --

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:11.023
balls.

00:03:11.023 --> 00:03:13.459
She's very ballsy.

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:16.362
She's very stubborn.
She's very controlling.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.464
And I really enjoyed,
you know --

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:21.100
I don't know that much
about religion per se.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.369
I was never brought up
with any religion.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:24.604
My mother was agnostic.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:27.673
My dad never really
made it to church.

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:30.443
So I wanted to sort of
understand more about

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:34.313
what it would be like
to be an evangelical.

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:36.716
And I didn't want to do
the Tammy Faye routine.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.018
I wanted to make her
much more complex than that,

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:41.354
which I hopefully think
we have achieved.

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:43.423
-And I know you are
a producer on this, as well.

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:46.391
Is it more interesting
when you get to have

00:03:46.391 --> 00:03:48.060
a say in the character

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.996
and make those choices
from behind the scenes, as well?

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:54.099
-Oh, it is. I mean, I'm --
I'm a woman of a certain age.

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:57.069
And I didn't want
to dumb her down at all.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:04:00.406
And I love strong,
powerful women.

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:04.310
So I wanted to oversee that.
I wanted to have a say in that.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.813
I produced another show
called "Sensitive Skin,"

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:09.882
and that was my first
producing outing.

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.384
And I -- I just loved it.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.721
I really, really enjoyed
the creative elements of it.

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:16.555
I didn't have anything to do
with the raising of the money

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:17.890
or anything like that,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.358
which I don't think
I'd be as good at.

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:22.295
But what --
Creatively, it was --

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:26.532
it was definitely an avenue
that I want to pursue further.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:28.701
-I've heard you say that
you were drawn to the character

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:32.171
because it wasn't like anything
you'd seen before.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.106
I have to imagine, as an actor,
it's so exciting

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.608
when you're looking
through a script

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.476
and you have that realization

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:42.281
that this is not a version of
something you're familiar with.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:44.583
-Absolutely.
Especially in your sixties.

00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:46.853
You know, I played
my first grandmother role

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:48.754
about a year and a half ago.

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:51.791
And, you know, I wanted
very much to expound on that

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:54.260
because, as you know,
you get older,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.061
you have so many
more stories to tell.

00:04:56.061 --> 00:04:59.031
You have so much more to give,
as far as information.

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:02.935
Not just about yourself
but what that age, you know,

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:06.572
entitles you to
and the challenges of it.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:10.142
But for me, I love the camp
element of "Filthy Rich."

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:14.347
You know, I love the fact
that I could be that theatrical

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:16.783
but also be very subtle
at the same time.

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:20.352
This character was very broad
but very intricately drawn.

00:05:20.352 --> 00:05:23.756
And, so, I read the script
that Tate Taylor,

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:27.259
the executive producer
and creator of it, sent me.

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:31.497
And the first question
that I asked him was,

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.998
"What happens next?"

00:05:32.998 --> 00:05:35.768
Which is always a really good
sign that there's --

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:37.403
you're in new territory.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:39.371
-You shoot this show
in New Orleans.

00:05:39.371 --> 00:05:43.008
And I would say food is
a really nice part of the show.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.576
Especially, the idea of, like,

00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:47.180
how food is a communal
experience with family.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.513
-Yes.
-And I guess family

00:05:48.513 --> 00:05:49.849
is the only place left

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:52.952
we're having
communal food experiences.

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.920
-Or any kind of communal.
-I know, right?

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:56.755
And then, at some point,
you realize,

00:05:56.755 --> 00:05:58.791
"Oh, there's a reason
I went out with people

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.192
that weren't my family."

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:03.229
-Yeah. Exactly.
-You sort of --

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.231
Obviously,
it's a great city for food.

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:08.100
And you pulled in a bunch
of restaurants and chefs

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.403
to be involved
in the making of the show.

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:13.939
Were they enthusiastic
to be part of it?

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:15.374
-Oh, my God, yes.

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:17.443
I mean, and there's --
there's so much to the cuisine.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:19.244
I had no idea
what a hummingbird cake was.

00:06:19.244 --> 00:06:21.781
I'd never heard of a hummingbird
cake or any of those things.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:26.486
So, I'd been to New Orleans,
but I never really experienced

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:28.620
what it's really like
to live there.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:29.922
We were -- In the first season,

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:32.925
we were there for about
seven months all in total.

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:34.994
And it's -- it's friendly.

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:37.663
It's welcoming.
It's crazy and zany.

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:40.733
But it also has this
little bit of danger to it,

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:43.169
and I'm not just talking spices.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:46.973
-Lastly, I did not
know this about you.

00:06:46.973 --> 00:06:49.007
This was a real
unknown fact for me.

00:06:49.007 --> 00:06:52.244
That you are a fan
of the Premier League.

00:06:52.244 --> 00:06:54.046
Not only that, but you --

00:06:54.046 --> 00:06:55.714
A second congratulations
in order.

00:06:55.714 --> 00:06:59.084
You support Liverpool, who won
the Premier League last year.

00:06:59.084 --> 00:07:00.352
Congratulations again.

00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:02.321
-We are -- We are the champions.
Thank you.

00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:04.056
Now, I just have
a question for you.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:07.260
How did West Ham happen?
-Yeah.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.627
It's a really good question.

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:11.930
I was -- This is a true story,
though -- I was in --

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:15.001
I was doing a show in London
in late -- in the late '90s.

00:07:15.001 --> 00:07:17.170
And I wanted to see
a Premier League game.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:21.073
And I went to a ticket agent,
and it was that or Arsenal.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:24.343
And you could argue
I made a terrible choice.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.578
-Well, I think you made
two terrible choices.

00:07:26.578 --> 00:07:28.447
-[ Laughs ] But I think --

00:07:28.447 --> 00:07:31.817
I think I deserve credit
for sticking with my bad choice

00:07:31.817 --> 00:07:33.285
because --
-You do. You do.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:34.720
That's commendable.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.722
You know,
when you are born in Liverpool

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:38.957
and both of your parents
are Liverpudlian,

00:07:38.957 --> 00:07:41.060
you're either for Everton
or you're a Red.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:43.595
And my dad was a Red.
So you have no choice.

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:44.997
-You had no choice.

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:46.798
I have a very similar thing
with my father and --

00:07:46.798 --> 00:07:48.834
and the Pittsburgh Steelers.
I will say this.

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:50.736
West Ham is playing Everton
on Wednesday.

00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:54.807
So maybe you could
at least root for me, then.

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:56.675
-Okay.
-[ Laughter ]

00:07:56.675 --> 00:07:58.878
-How dare you. You know what?
You're a bad winner.

00:07:58.878 --> 00:08:01.714
You're the champion,
and you should be nicer
to people like me.

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:05.517
-We've waited a long time.
-You -- And it's well-deserved.

00:08:05.517 --> 00:08:07.853
Thank you for being here.
It's lovely to talk to you.

00:08:07.853 --> 00:08:09.689
-Oh, pleasure. Thanks so much.

